Q: How do I make this code shorter and more efficient? A: <blockquote>
<p>How do I make this code shorter and more efficient?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Before trying any of the above, you must ensure that the program is correct.  Your program seems to work as expected but note these remarks:</p>
<ul>
<li>using a global variable <code>length</code> is unnecessary;</li>
<li>the function <code>check_length</code> uses a convoluted method to compute the length and does not always set <code>length</code>, furthermore the return value is not <em>checked</em>.</li>
<li><code>check_luhn()</code> is called twice;</li>
<li>the names of the local variables in <code>check_luhn()</code> are not explicit, making the code hard to follow.</li>
<li>the card number and length are passed in a different order to <code>check_company()</code> and <code>check_luhn()</code>: this is confusing and error prone.</li>
</ul>
<p>Here is a simplified program:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;cs50.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

// return the name of the card provider or NULL if unknown
static const char *get_company(long c) {
    // compute the number of digits in the card number
    int len = 3;  // assume at least 3 digits, keep 2 leading digits
    while ((c /= 10) &gt;= 100)
        len++;
    if (len == 15 &amp;&amp; (c == 34 || c == 37))
        return &quot;AMEX&quot;;
    if ((len == 13 || len == 16) &amp;&amp; (c &gt;= 40 &amp;&amp; c &lt;= 49))
        return &quot;VISA&quot;;
    if (len == 16 &amp;&amp; (c &gt;= 51 &amp;&amp; c &lt;= 55))
        return &quot;MASTERCARD&quot;;
    return NULL;
}

// return non zero if card number has valid Luhn checksum
static int check_luhn(long c) {
    int sum = 0;
    while (c) {
        int e = c % 100;    // handle 2 digits at a time
        c = c / 100;
        sum += e % 10;      // add digits at even positions
        e = e / 10 * 2;     // double digits at odd positions
        sum += e % 10 + e / 10;  // add sum of digits if greater then 9
    }
    return sum % 10 == 0;   // Luhn checksum must be a multiple of 10
}

int main(void) {
    long c = get_long(&quot;Enter credit card number: &quot;);
    const char *company;
    if (check_luhn(c) &amp;&amp; (company = get_company(c)) != NULL)
        printf(&quot;%s\n&quot;, company);
    else
        printf(&quot;INVALID\n&quot;);
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>

Q: Generics when alternating types in variable number of arguments to a function A: <p>Just wanted to post here what I finally implemented in the end. This solution is based on @RichardTowers answer.</p>
<pre><code>// Sort By Function
type SortByObjToValF&lt;refT&gt; = (ref: refT) =&gt; any
type SortByDirType = 'ASC' | 'DESC'
export function sortBy&lt;ObjT&gt;(...args: (SortByObjToValF&lt;ObjT&gt; | [SortByObjToValF&lt;ObjT&gt;, SortByDirType])[]) {
    let sortByOptions = args.map((arg): [SortByObjToValF&lt;ObjT&gt;, SortByDirType] =&gt; {
        return typeof arg == 'function' ? [arg, 'ASC'] : arg
    })
    return function (objA: ObjT, objB: ObjT) {
        for (let sortByOption of sortByOptions) {
            let valA = sortByOption[0](objA)
            let valB = sortByOption[0](objB)
            if (valA &lt; valB) return sortByOption[1] == 'ASC' ? -1 :  1
            if (valA &gt; valB) return sortByOption[1] == 'ASC' ?  1 : -1
        }
        return 0
    }
}

// Example Usage
let a = [
    {a: 1, b: 1, c: { d: 3 }},
    {a: 1, b: 2, c: { d: 2 }},
    {a: 3, b: 3, c: { d: 1 }}
].sort(sortBy(
    x=&gt;x.a,
    [x=&gt;x.b, 'DESC'],
    [x=&gt;x.c.d, 'ASC']
))
</code></pre>
